ognitive models overstates the case and unnecessarily feeds LLM hype.\nL17: Comments:  | 5 pages. This is an invited commentary under review at Behavioral and Brain Sciences\nL18: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL); Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)\nL19: Cite as:  | cite7†arXiv:2601.10421 [cs.CL]\nL20:    | (or cite8†arXiv:2601.10421v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL21:    | cite9†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.10421†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL22: ## Submission history\nL23: \nL24: From: Philip Resnik [cite10†view email ]\nL25: [v1] Thu, 15 Jan 2026 14:13:01 UTC (172 KB)\nL26: \nL27: Full-text links:\nL28: \nL29: ## Access Paper:\nL30: \nL31: View a PDF of the paper titled Are Language Models Models?, by Philip Resnik\nL32: \nL33:   * cite6†View PDF L34: \nL35: cite11†view license†creativecommons.org L36: \nL37: ### Current browse context:\nL38: \nL39: cs.CL\nL40: \nL41: cite12†< prev |   cite13†next > L42: \nL43: cite14†new | cite15†recent | cite16†2026-01 L44: \nL45: Change to browse by:\nL46: \nL47: cite17†cs L48: cite18†cs.AI L49: ### References & Citations\nL50: \nL51:   * cite19†NASA ADS†ui.adsabs.harvard.edu L52:   * cite20†Google Scholar†scholar.google.com L53:   * cite21†Semantic Scholar†api.semanticscholar.org L54: \nL55: [Button: export BibTeX citation] Loading...\nL56: \nL57: ## BibTeX formatted citation\nL58: \nL59: [Button: ×]\nL60: \nL61: loading...\nL62: \nL63: Data provided by:\nL64: \nL65: ### Bookmark\nL66: \nL67: [Input] Bibliographic Tools\nL68: # Bibliographic and Citation Tools\nL69: \nL70: [Input] Bibliographic Explorer Toggle\nL71: \nL72: Bibliographic Explorer (cite22†What is the Explorer?†info.arxiv.org )\nL73: \nL74: [Input] Connected Papers Toggle\nL75: \nL76: Connected Papers (cite23†What is Connected Papers?†www.connectedpapers.com )\nL77: \nL78: [Input] Litmaps Toggle\nL79: \nL80: Litmaps (cite24†What is Litmaps?†www.litmaps.co )\nL81: \nL82: [Input] sci