pted our values of openness, community, excellence, and user data privacy. arXiv is committed to these values and only works with partners that adhere to them.\nL149: Have an idea for a project that will add value for arXiv's community? cite37†Learn more about arXivLabs†info.arxiv.org .\nL150: \nL151: cite38†Which authors of this paper are endorsers? | Disable MathJax (cite39†What is MathJax?†info.arxiv.org )\nL152: \nL153: We gratefully acknowledge support from our major funders, cite40†member institutions†info.arxiv.org , , and all contributors.\nL154: cite41†About†info.arxiv.org · cite42†Help†info.arxiv.org · cite43†Contact†info.arxiv.org · cite44†Subscribe†info.arxiv.org · cite45†Copyright†info.arxiv.org · cite46†Privacy†info.arxiv.org · cite47†Accessibility†info.arxiv.org · cite48†Operational Status (opens in new tab)†status.arxiv.org L155: \nL156: Major funding support from\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n[2601.10660v1] Detecting Winning Arguments with Large Language Models and Persuasion Strategies (https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.10660v1)\nciteturn30856view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 3 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2601.10660v1\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 159\nL0: cite0†Search cite1†Submit cite2†Donate†info.arxiv.org cite3†Log in L1: \nL2: Search arXiv [Input: Search papers by title, author, abstract, or ID...] [Input] [Input]\nL3: \nL4: Press Enter to search · cite4†Advanced search L5: \nL6: # Computer Science > Computation and Language\nL7: \nL8: [Submitted on 15 Jan 2026]\nL9: # Title:Detecting Winning Arguments with Large Language Models and Persuasion Strategies\nL10: \nL11: Authors:cite5†Tiziano Labruna , cite6†Arkadiusz Modzelewski , cite7†Giorgio Satta , cite8†Giovanni Da San Martino L12: \nL13: View a PDF of the paper titled Detecting Winning Arguments with Large Language Models and Persuasion Strategies, by Tiziano Labruna and 3 other authors\nL14: \nL15: cite9†View PDF cite10†HTML (experimental) L16: > Abstract:Detecting persuasion in argumentative text is a challenging task with important implications for understanding human communication. This work investigates the role of persuasion strategies - such as Attack on reputation, Distraction, and Manipulative wording - in determining the persuasiveness of a text. We conduct experiments on three annotated argument datasets: Winning Arguments (built from the Change My View subreddit), Anthropic/Persuasion, and Persuasion for Good.\nL17: Our approach leverages large language models (LLMs) with a Multi-Strategy Persuasion Scoring approach that guides reasoning over six persuasion strategies. Results show that strategy-guided reasoning improves the prediction of persuasiveness. To better understand the influence of content, we organize the Winning Argument dataset into broad discussion topics and analyze performance across them. We publicly release this topic-annotated version of the dataset to facilitate future research.\nL18: Overall, our methodology demonstrates the value of structured, strategy-aware prompting for enhancing interpretability and robustness in argument quality assessment.\nL19: Subjects:  | Computation and Language (cs.CL)\nL20: Cite as:  | cite11†arXiv:2601.10660 [cs.CL]\nL21:    | (or cite12†arXiv:2601.10660v1 [cs.CL] for this version)\nL22:    | cite13†https://doi.org/10.48550/arXiv.2601.10660†doi.org arXiv-issued DOI via DataCite\nL23: ## Submission history\nL24: \nL25: From: Tiziano Labruna [cite14†view email ]\nL26: [v1] Thu, 15 Jan 2026 18:30:15 UTC (350 KB)\nL27: \nL28: Full-text links:\nL29: \nL30: ## Access Paper:\nL31: \nL32: View a PDF of the paper titled Detecting Winning Arguments with Large Language Models and Persuasion Strategies, by Tiziano Labruna and 3 other authors\nL33: \nL34:   * cite9†View PDF L35:   * cite10†HTML (experimental) L36